// Configuration for airspeed velocity, the historical benchmarking tool.
//
// This file is the counterpart to the harness in benchmarks/__init__.py, and
// the two answer different questions. The harness measures the working tree
// now, which is what a contributor investigating a routine and the
// continuous integration pipeline both want. This configuration measures the
// history: it checks out a range of commits, builds each one in its own
// environment, runs the suite against every one, and produces a browsable
// record of how the library's performance moved over time.
//
// Use the harness for a question about the present:
//
//     python -m benchmarks --group graphs --filter dijkstra
//
// Use this configuration for a question about the past:
//
//     asv run ALL --config benchmarks/asv.conf.json
//     asv run v1.0.0..main --config benchmarks/asv.conf.json
//     asv continuous main HEAD --config benchmarks/asv.conf.json
//     asv compare v1.0.0 main --config benchmarks/asv.conf.json
//     asv publish --config benchmarks/asv.conf.json
//     asv preview --config benchmarks/asv.conf.json
//
// The two tools share their declarations. Every benchmark is declared once,
// with the decorator in benchmarks/__init__.py, and the adapter in
// benchmarks/asv_suite/suite.py generates the discoverable classes this tool
// needs from that single declaration. Nothing is measured here that the
// harness does not also measure, and nothing is declared twice.
//
// Note that airspeed velocity is an optional development dependency,
// installed through the pip section of environment.yml rather than through
// any extra of the library. Nothing in this file is required in order to
// use discretus, and nothing in the library imports it.
//
// Comments are permitted here because airspeed velocity strips them before
// parsing, which is the format its own generated template uses. They must
// occupy a whole line: the configuration linting job in the lint workflow
// strips whole line comments in the same way before validating this file,
// and a trailing comment would therefore survive the strip and break the
// parse. Keeping the rule identical in both places means a file that parses
// locally parses in the pipeline.
{
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // Identity
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------

    // The version of the configuration format. One is current.
    "version": 1,

    // The project name, which becomes the title of the published report.
    "project": "discretus",

    // Where a reader of the report is sent for the project itself.
    "project_url": "https://github.com/olaflaitinen/discretus",

    // How a commit hash in the report links back to the forge. The trailing
    // slash matters: the tool appends the hash to this string without
    // inserting a separator.
    "show_commit_url": "https://github.com/olaflaitinen/discretus/commit/",

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // What to measure
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------

    // The repository to measure. The path is relative to this file, so the
    // two levels up reach the repository root. An absolute path or a remote
    // address also works, and the relative form is used here so that a
    // clone measures itself rather than whatever is on a maintainer's disk.
    "repo": "../",

    // The importable package lives at the repository root rather than in a
    // source subdirectory, so this stays empty.
    "repo_subdir": "",

    // The branches to consider when a commit range is not given. Only the
    // default branch is measured by default, because a feature branch is
    // measured through the continuous command against its base instead.
    "branches": ["main"],

    // The version control system. Naming it explicitly saves a probe.
    "dvcs": "git",

    // Where the benchmark modules live, relative to this file.
    //
    // This points at the adapter directory rather than at the suite itself,
    // and the reason is worth recording because the obvious alternative
    // fails. Airspeed velocity imports every module it finds under this
    // directory as a top level module, with the directory placed on the
    // import path. The bench modules reach the harness through relative
    // imports, which require a parent package, so importing them that way
    // raises an import error on every one of them. Pointing this at the
    // benchmarks directory would therefore discover nothing and report
    // seven import failures.
    //
    // The adapter avoids the problem rather than working around it. It is
    // alone in its directory, so it is the only module imported here, and
    // it puts the repository root on the import path and then imports the
    // harness by its proper name. The harness imports the bench modules as
    // the package submodules they are.
    "benchmark_dir": "./asv_suite",

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // Environments
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------

    // How the environments are created. The virtualenv backend is used
    // rather than conda, because the library has no compiled parts and no
    // system dependencies, so the lighter backend is sufficient and is far
    // faster to create. A contributor who prefers conda can override this
    // on the command line with the environment type option.
    "environment_type": "virtualenv",

    // Seconds allowed for an installation. The default is a minute, which is
    // generous for a package with no dependencies and tight for a first run
    // that has to download the interpreter's own build tooling.
    "install_timeout": 600,

    // The channels to use if the conda backend is selected on the command
    // line. Recorded rather than omitted so that the override works without
    // a second flag, and ordered with the community channel first because
    // that is where the current interpreter builds are.
    "conda_channels": ["conda-forge", "defaults"],

    // The environment file to use if the conda backend is selected. Left
    // unset, because environment.yml at the repository root describes a
    // development environment, including the documentation and linting
    // tools, and installing all of that into every measured commit would
    // multiply the runtime of a historical run for no benefit. The
    // dependency matrix above is the right place to name what a measurement
    // actually needs.
    "conda_environment_file": null,

    // The interpreter versions to measure. The oldest and the newest
    // supported versions are both listed, because the library's performance
    // profile differs between them: the newer interpreters have faster
    // attribute access and a faster call sequence, which matters for a pure
    // Python library whose hot paths are interpreter bound loops.
    //
    // PyPy is deliberately absent here. It is measured by the interpreters
    // job of the benchmark workflow instead, because a historical record
    // that mixes implementations is harder to read than two separate
    // records, and because the just in time compiler makes the warm up
    // behaviour different enough to deserve its own treatment.
    "pythons": ["3.9", "3.12"],

    // The dependency matrix. The library requires nothing at runtime, which
    // is the whole point of the design, so the only entries are the
    // optional visualization backends and they are measured in one
    // configuration rather than across versions.
    //
    // An empty string as a version means the dependency is absent in that
    // configuration, which is how the no extras configuration is expressed.
    "matrix": {
        "req": {
            "matplotlib": ["", "3.8"],
            "graphviz": [""]
        },
        "env": {
            // A fixed hash seed removes one source of run to run variation
            // in anything that touches a dictionary or a set. The library
            // sorts its output through a total order so that results do not
            // depend on hash values, and fixing the seed removes the
            // remaining variation in the measurement itself.
            "PYTHONHASHSEED": ["0"],
            // The library reads its configuration from the environment.
            // Fixing the seed here makes every randomized routine
            // reproducible across the whole historical run, which is what
            // makes two commits comparable at all. The value is the one the
            // harness uses as its base seed, so a measurement taken here
            // and a measurement taken through the harness see the same
            // inputs.
            "DISCRETUS_DEFAULT_SEED": ["20260115"],
            // Strict validation is left on, because it is the default a user
            // gets and therefore the configuration worth measuring. Turning
            // it off would make the axiom verification benchmarks measure
            // nothing, and those are among the few routines whose cost a
            // user is likely to be surprised by.
            "DISCRETUS_STRICT_VALIDATION": ["true"],
            // Deterministic output ordering is also a default, and it is
            // the reason several routines sort where a less careful library
            // would not. Measuring with it off would understate the cost of
            // the library a user actually installs.
            "DISCRETUS_DETERMINISTIC": ["true"]
        },
        "env_nobuild": {}
    },

    // Combinations to leave out of the matrix. The visualization backends
    // are not measured on the oldest interpreter, because the historical
    // record of the rendering code is less interesting than the record of
    // the computational core and the extra configurations double the
    // runtime of a full historical run.
    "exclude": [
        {
            "python": "3.9",
            "req": {"matplotlib": "3.8"}
        }
    ],

    // Combinations to add that the matrix would not generate. None are
    // needed at present; the entry is kept so that adding one later is a
    // change to a value rather than a change to the structure.
    "include": [],

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // Build and install
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------

    // The commands used to build and install the package into each
    // environment. They are stated explicitly rather than left to the
    // default, because the default assumes a setup.py invocation and this
    // project builds through pyproject.toml.
    //
    // The wheel is built once per commit and cached, then installed into
    // every environment for that commit, which is why the build command
    // writes into the build cache directory and the install command reads
    // the wheel back out of it.
    "build_command": [
        "python -m pip install build",
        "python -m build --wheel -o {build_cache_dir} {build_dir}"
    ],
    "install_command": [
        "python -m pip install {wheel_file}"
    ],

    // The uninstall is allowed to fail, which is what the return code
    // prefix expresses. It runs before the first install into a fresh
    // environment, where there is nothing to uninstall, and treating that
    // as an error would abort every run.
    "uninstall_command": [
        "return-code=any python -m pip uninstall -y discretus"
    ],

    // Whether to cache the built package between commits. Enabled with a
    // small cache, because building a pure Python package is cheap and the
    // cache mostly saves the environment resolution rather than a
    // compilation. A larger cache would mostly consume disk.
    "build_cache_size": 4,

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // Where output goes
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------

    // Where the created environments are cached. Kept out of the repository
    // by .gitignore, because an environment directory is large and is
    // reproducible from this file.
    "env_dir": "../.asv/env",

    // Where the raw measurements are written. These are the files worth
    // keeping: the published report is derived from them and can always be
    // regenerated, while a lost measurement means re running the history.
    "results_dir": "../.asv/results",

    // Where the browsable report is generated. This one is genuinely
    // disposable, and the publish command rewrites it from the results.
    "html_dir": "../.asv/html",

    // Length of the commit hashes shown in the report. Eight is enough to be
    // unambiguous in a repository of this size and short enough to read.
    "hash_length": 8,

    // Plugins to load. None are needed: the suite uses the plain timing
    // benchmarks that airspeed velocity provides, and the harness in
    // benchmarks/__init__.py provides the reporting that a plugin would
    // otherwise add.
    "plugins": [],

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // Limits
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------

    // The default time limit for a single benchmark, in seconds. The suite
    // contains routines that are exponential by nature, namely exact graph
    // colouring, Hamiltonicity, satisfiability, and exhaustive enumeration,
    // and the sizes are chosen so that each finishes well inside this
    // limit. A benchmark that exceeds it is reported as a failure rather
    // than allowed to occupy a runner, which is the right outcome: it means
    // either a regression or a size that was chosen too generously.
    //
    // The adapter repeats this value as a class attribute, because a
    // generated class does not inherit the configured default. The two are
    // kept in step by hand, and a disagreement between them is harmless in
    // one direction only: a smaller value on the class wins.
    "default_benchmark_timeout": 120,

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // Regression detection
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------

    // The empty key applies to every benchmark and says that a change is
    // only reported when it exceeds a factor of one and a half, which is
    // well outside the resolution of a shared machine. A tighter threshold
    // would fill the report with noise and train a reader to ignore it.
    //
    // The remaining keys are tighter, at a factor of one fifth, and each is
    // tighter for the same reason: the reference makes a specific
    // asymptotic claim about that routine, so a smaller change is worth
    // investigating even though it is closer to the noise floor. They are
    // the routines a user is most likely to have in a loop, and the ones
    // where a change of implementation strategy, rather than a change of
    // constant factor, is the likely cause of a step in the curve.
    //
    // The identifiers are those the adapter generates, that is the module
    // name, the benchmark name, and the timed method. The benchmark names
    // are the same ones the harness accepts as a filter, so a regression
    // reported here can be reproduced with
    //
    //     python -m benchmarks --filter <name>
    //
    // Every identifier below corresponds to a declaration that exists. A
    // threshold for a benchmark that does not exist is silently ignored by
    // the tool, which is the worst possible outcome, so the list is checked
    // against the generated identifiers with
    //
    //     python benchmarks/asv_suite/suite.py
    "regressions_thresholds": {
        "": 0.5,

        // Set algebra. The most frequently called code in the library, and
        // linear in the total size of its arguments, so a step here is
        // almost certainly a change of the underlying representation.
        "suite.union_integers.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.intersection_integers.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.union_bit_sets.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.intersection_bit_sets.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.membership.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.construction.time_run": 0.2,

        // Relations and equivalences. The closure is the cubic routine a
        // user is most likely to call on something large, and the union
        // find operations claim near constant amortized cost, which is the
        // kind of claim that quietly stops holding.
        "suite.transitive_closure.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.is_transitive_sparse.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.union_find_merge.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.union_find_find.time_run": 0.2,

        // Logic. The Tseitin transformation is documented as linear in the
        // size of the formula, which is the property that makes encoding to
        // conjunctive normal form practical at all.
        "suite.tseitin_nested.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.truth_table.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.unit_propagation.time_run": 0.2,

        // Combinatorics. The counting routines are the ones called in a
        // loop, and the prefix generators claim constant time to produce a
        // first object from an exponential space, which is the whole point
        // of the lazy enumeration.
        "suite.binomial_middle.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.catalan.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.stirling_second.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.permutations_prefix.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.power_set_prefix.time_run": 0.2,

        // Graphs. Traversal and shortest paths dominate any real use of the
        // package, and each of these has a documented bound that a change
        // of data structure would break without changing any result.
        "suite.adjacency_list.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.connected_components.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.topological_sort.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.tarjan_strong_components.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.bfs_shortest.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.dijkstra_sparse.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.dijkstra_dense.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.kruskal_sparse.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.prim_sparse.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.greedy_coloring.time_run": 0.2,

        // Number theory. The modular arithmetic and the sieve are the
        // routines most likely to sit inside someone else's loop, and the
        // greatest common divisor is called by nearly everything else.
        "suite.extended_euclidean.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.binary_gcd.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.modular_exponentiation.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.modular_inverse.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.sieve_eratosthenes.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.miller_rabin_on_prime.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.euler_totient_from_factorization.time_run": 0.2,

        // Algebra. The axiom verification is quadratic or cubic by
        // necessity, and it runs at construction, so it is the cost a user
        // is most often surprised by and the one most worth watching.
        "suite.core_verify_closure.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.core_verify_associativity.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.core_identity_search.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.cyclic_group_unverified.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.polynomial_multiply.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.polynomial_divmod.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.polynomial_evaluate.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.core_matrix_multiply.time_run": 0.2,
        "suite.core_matrix_power.time_run": 0.2,

        // Peak memory is reported for the whole process and therefore
        // includes the prepared input, so the figures are coarse. The
        // threshold is correspondingly loose: it is meant to catch a
        // routine that began materialising a structure it used to stream,
        // not to police an allocation or two.
        "suite.union_integers.peakmem_run": 1.0,
        "suite.transitive_closure.peakmem_run": 1.0,
        "suite.power_set_prefix.peakmem_run": 1.0,
        "suite.permutations_prefix.peakmem_run": 1.0,
        "suite.adjacency_list.peakmem_run": 1.0,
        "suite.sieve_eratosthenes.peakmem_run": 1.0,

        // The availability counts are not timings. They rise as the library
        // is built out, and a rise is not a regression, so the threshold is
        // set high enough that only a fall to nothing would be reported.
        // A genuine fall, meaning a routine that used to be measurable and
        // is not, shows up in the report as a step in the plot and is worth
        // investigating by hand.
        "suite.Availability.track_available": 10.0,
        "suite.Availability.track_declared": 10.0
    },

    // Where regression detection starts. The first stable release is the
    // baseline, because measurements from before it describe a library whose
    // interface was still moving and are not comparable.
    "regressions_first_commits": {
        "": "v1.0.0"
    }
}

// Keys deliberately left at their defaults
// =======================================
//
// The tool accepts several settings that are absent above. Each is absent
// for a reason, and recording the reason here is cheaper than rediscovering
// it the next time someone wonders whether the file is incomplete.
//
// The benchmark attribute defaults, that is the repetition count, the sample
// count, the warm up time, and the number of processes, are not set at the
// configuration level. They are set per benchmark by the adapter, from the
// declaration in the bench module, because the right number of repetitions
// for a sieve over ten million integers is not the right number for a
// membership test. A configuration level default would be overridden in
// every single case.
//
// The branch list holds only the default branch. Adding a release branch
// would make the report show two histories that diverge and rejoin, which
// is accurate and unreadable. A release branch is measured on its own, with
// an explicit commit range.
//
// No plugin is loaded. The tool's plugin interface exists to add benchmark
// types and reporting, and both are already covered: the timing and peak
// memory types are built in, and the reporting a contributor actually reads
// comes from the harness, which prints growth ratios and implied exponents
// that this tool does not compute.
//
// How this file is validated
// ==========================
//
// Three things can be wrong with it, and each has a check.
//
// It can fail to parse. The configuration linting job in the lint workflow
// strips whole line comments and parses what is left, along with every other
// configuration file in the repository, so a malformed edit fails the
// pipeline rather than the next historical run.
//
// It can name a benchmark that does not exist. A threshold whose key matches
// nothing is silently ignored by the tool, which means a routine the
// reference makes a promise about would quietly stop being watched. The
// adapter prints every identifier it generates:
//
//     python benchmarks/asv_suite/suite.py
//
// It can drift from the harness. The two tools must see the same inputs, or
// a measurement taken through one cannot be compared with a measurement
// taken through the other. The seed in the environment matrix above is the
// harness base seed, and the timeout is the harness timeout; both are
// duplicated here because the tool cannot read them from the harness, and
// both are worth checking when a number from the two sources disagrees by
// more than the machine explains.
//
// Notes on reading the historical report
// ======================================
//
// A step in a curve is the interesting shape. It means one commit changed
// the cost, and the report names the commit, so the question becomes whether
// the change was intended. A gradual drift across many commits is almost
// always the machine rather than the library: a historical run can span days
// of wall clock time and several runner generations.
//
// A curve that begins partway along the history has not regressed from
// nothing. It begins where the routine was first implemented. The two
// availability counts in the thresholds above exist to make that explicit,
// because a timing report alone cannot distinguish a routine that was
// removed from one that had not yet been written.
//
// A change of slope between two sizes of the same benchmark matters more
// than a change of absolute value at one size. The reference documents
// asymptotic bounds, and an asymptotic bound is a statement about slope. The
// harness reports the implied exponent directly and is the better tool for
// that question. This one is the better tool for the question of when the
// slope changed.
//
// A regression reported at a single size, on a single interpreter, in a
// single environment of the matrix, is usually noise. A regression that
// appears at every size of one benchmark, or at one size of every benchmark
// in a package, is usually real, and the shape of the pattern is the first
// clue about the cause.
//
// The peak memory figures cover the whole process and therefore include the
// prepared input, which for the larger sizes dominates them. They are useful
// for finding the commit at which a routine began materialising a structure
// it used to stream, and useless as an estimate of what a routine costs on
// its own.
